WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.823
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.823 --> 00:00:09.400
You may be surprised but the most popular

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:16.040
algorithms for finding motifs in DNA
sequences, roll dice

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.249
and take chances to find such motifs.
They're called randomized algorithms.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.288
How do they work?

00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:29.050
We have now learned how to construct Profile
given

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.420
Motifs, but can we construct Motifs given
Profile?

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.760
We can, because given an arbitrary Profile
and a set of sequences Dna,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:47.430
we can construct the set Motifs(Profile,DNA)
by simply finding

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:53.330
Profile-most probable k-mers in each
sequence from Dna.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:01.650
And therefore, we can further iterate this
process by finding profile of motifs,

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:09.100
motifs of profiles, profile of motifs,
until our motifs keep improving.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:14.030
So this is the key idea of the algorithm
called RandomizedMotifSearch.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.910
We simply iterate and see whether the
score of motif continues

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.230
to improve. And stop when it doesn't
improve anymore.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.530
The only question about this
algorithm is

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.710
how in the world it can find anything
useful.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.920
Think about this, we started from
a completely random motifs.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.528
If the motifs are completely random, 

00:01:42.528 --> 00:01:45.726
and if they're taken from random
sequences,

00:01:45.726 --> 00:01:49.580
then presumably. expected probability of
every element

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.120
in the profile matrix is 1/4.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.797
There is absolutely no information in such
a motif.

00:01:56.797 --> 00:02:02.130
How possibly it can lead us to the correct
motif in the sequence?

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:10.070
Let's just keep our fingers crossed and
see how this approach works on an example.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.110
Here's my example.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:15.568
These are very short DNA strings and I
implanted

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:20.620
4-nucleotide long motifs (one motif on each string).
They're shown in blue, so

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.540
ACGT is implanted in every sequence with a
single mutation.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.810
So how randomized motif search will
proceed?

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.300
First, it will randomly select motifs
shown in bold here.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.460
And, of course, it has missed most
of the motifs.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.470
They are randomly selected.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.970
Afterwards, it will form Motifs matrix,
shown here on

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.720
the slide, and afterwards it will
construct Profile(Motifs).

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.180
Using Profile(Motifs) it will calculate
the probability

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.410
of every k-mer in the set of strings,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:07.900
shown here, and will simply select the most
probable k-mers in every string.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:13.610
The results are in this matrix, and I
should probably start iterating.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.390
But if you look carefully at this matrix,
I don't have to iterate.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.910
It already found all blue inserted motifs
right there.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:27.920
It almost sounds like a magic

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.090
trick, because we started from something
completely random

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.860
and, in a single iteration, found our
implanted motifs!

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:36.450
Well, what happened?

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.290
So let's try to think about this.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:47.310
When we form k-mers randomly, it results
in a uniform expected profile,

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:53.570
as we discussed before.
But strings in Dna are not random

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.800
because w have inserted implanted
patterns 

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.440
in our Dna strings.
Therefore,  this implanted motif

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.870
resulted in a biased profile;
it's not a completely random profile.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.690
Where does this statistical bias point to?
If it points

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:20.080
in the direction of the implanted motif, then
there is no miracle

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.160
that we start from something random (which
is not really random), 

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:30.430
and, applying iterative procedure, arrived to a
hidden part.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.530
So, let's see how it actually works.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.390
I return back to the case that we
considered before.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.590
You saw that when we randomly selected

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.160
motifs, in the first sequence, we missed
the motif (the correct, the blue one).

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.160
In the second sequence, we also missed it.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.200
In the third sequnce, we missed it.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.420
In the fourth sequence, we missed it.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.230
But in the fifth one, simply by pure
chance, we actually found it.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.323
It's not a big deal.

00:04:57.323 --> 00:05:01.790
There is always a probability that we
simply by chance

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:05.134
select one of the implanted motifs.

00:05:05.134 --> 00:05:09.410
But it results in a biased profile.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.640
If you look at the profiles that

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.600
resulted from this implantation, then you
see that the largest elements

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.630
in this profile actually correspond to
A, C, G and T

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:26.720
of our implanted pattern ACGT.
Therefore, even

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.880
a single correctly found k-mer in

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.010
the set of Dna strings creates a bias in
the profile, and this bias

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:42.610
leads us to the correct sequence.
Of course, it doesn't always happen.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.090
It depends on how we have chosen initial

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:49.380
set of randomly selected k-mers in the
sequence.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.250
But if we try this algorithm, a thousand
or

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.410
a million times, there is a good chance
that

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.650
eventually one of these attempts will
bring us to

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.879
the correct motif. Even if this procedure
sounds ridiculous.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:12.880
We now move to the next topic:  
"How do bacteria hibernate?"

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:20.050
Our goal is to apply our motifs finding algorithm
to real biological problems.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.954
To figure out how our algorithms work on

00:06:22.954 --> 00:06:28.640
real biological data, we will start by
analyzing tuberculosis.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:31.620
Tuberculosis is a deadly disease that

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.080
killed quarter of population of Europe in
the 19th century.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.710
Unfortunately, humanity is not done 
with tuberculosis yet since

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:47.990
tuberculosis strains that resist
to all antibiotics are now emerging.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.560
Tuberculosis is caused by

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.000
Mycobacterium tuberculosis also called
MTB.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.500
An interesting thing about MTB

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:01.970
is that it can persist in for decades in humans

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:08.300
and one third of the world population is
affected by MTB.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:12.930
Of course, it usually doesn't lead to
tuberculosis

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.760
nut, in some cases. bacteria switches
from its latent

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.600
state to the active state and 
it's when the tuberculosis begins. 

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.030
The question that we will be asking is how

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:32.280
MTB switches from the latent to the active state
and how

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:38.260
does it survive during latency?
MTB has an ability

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:43.540
to form dormant spores where metabolism is
shut down.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:48.000
In such sporulated form it can survive
in

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.490
very tough conditions, for example, in
conditions of food shortage or

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:57.580
oxygen shortage.
And oxygen shortage, called hypoxia, is

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:03.440
associated with these latent forms of
tuberculosis.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.250
So, how can we figure out what makes

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:13.000
MTB switch into the latent state and
return back to

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.310
the active states?

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:20.140
Biologists have performed a
DNA array experiment and identified

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:26.410
roughly 25 genes that are activated in the
hypoxic condition.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.480
So when bacteria is deprived from

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.030
oxygen, these gene dramatically change
their expression.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.465
And our goal today is to discover what
regulatory

00:08:38.465 --> 00:08:43.609
motif controls these genes when they're
activated or repressed.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:49.110
We now have quite an arsenal of algorithms
for finding motifs.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.530
Let's try them.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:58.950
But the first problem is that we have
no clue of what size of motif to try.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:03.530
Should we try k equal to 8 or k equal to 20?
Let's try all of them

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:07.070
using median string problem and randomized
motif search.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.900
And you will see that, for different k,
we are getting quite different results.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.320
It is not clear which one to choose.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.260
for median string, we actually will have

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.600
difficulties going beyond k=12
because 

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.170
it is an exponential algorithm whose
running time grows as four to the power k.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.520
But for randomized motif search we can
search

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.100
for very long motif and, for k=20,

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:37.940
we actually find this rather long
motif.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:39.665
You can see that there are
similarities

00:09:39.665 --> 00:09:43.395
Between the 12-mer found by the Median String.

00:09:43.395 --> 00:09:47.570
the 12-mer found by the RandomizedMotifSearch, 

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:53.200
and the 20-mer found by the RandomizedMotifSearch.
Which one is correct?

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:54.170
To answer this

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:58.540
question, we need to introduce the notion
of the motif entropy.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.250
And entropy of motif is defined in the
following way.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:06.870
First, we construct the profile matrix.

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:13.820
Every column of the profile matrix correspond
to a biased four-sided dice.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.240
Or, in other words, to a probability
distribution, (p1, p2, ...p_n).

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.276
Sum of p_i is equal

00:10:19.276 --> 00:10:24.510
to 1, of course.
And entropy is simply defined as

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:29.350
- p_i * log (p_i)

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:34.350
and summed up over all indices i.
For example, we can compute the entropy of

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:39.445
the first column, and it will be 1.147.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.280
We can compare it with the minimal

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:49.740
possible entropy, which corresponds to the
extremely conserved motif

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:55.050
(where all symbols in the column are the
same), 

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.864
and with the maximal entropy 

00:10:57.864 --> 00:11:00.870
(where symbola in the column are equally likely).

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:06.730
So the entropy of a motif is defined as
the sum of the entropies of its columns.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:10.070
And now, the moment of truth.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:13.030
Let's see how the algorithms that we
developed

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:21.080
find out whether the motifs that we find
are close to the biological one.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:28.820
Here is the motif logo of the dormancy
survival regulator site.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:34.230
The motif logo is constructed in such a
way that the total length of four

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:40.880
nucleotides in the motif logo correspond
to two minus entropy.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.430
So for completely conserved column, like
nucleotide G

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:48.900
in this motif, we will have height two.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:52.850
It is the most conserved
columns of the motif.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:57.230
The relative sizes of 
four nucleotide in

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:02.140
each position correspond to frequencies of
these nucleotides in the column.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.020
This is an example of

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:06.620
motif logo.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.650
Now, let's see how it works for
the tuberculosis example.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:14.030
You can see that our randomized motif

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:18.800
search has actually captured a good portion of
the motif.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.910
But you can also see that we missed in
some places.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.700
We need to work harder and to
figure

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:31.320
out what to do to develop better
regulatory motif finding algorithms.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:36.901
We move next to another randomized
algorithm called Gibbs sampler.

